{
  "claims": [
    {
      "component_id": "C1",
      "text": "Traditional MRI methodologies require full k-space information, which follows a unique data acquisition sequence to reconstruct the complete image, and this process presents a time-consuming obstacle for medical imaging techniques. To address this, one study proposed a novel image reconstruction method intended to enhance the efficiency of data acquisition while maintaining high accuracy in activation detection, combining through-plane and in-plane acceleration techniques to accelerate image acquisition along two dimensions, and using multiple image-shift strategies together with a 2D Hadamard encoding scheme to increase encoding diversity and reduce slice leakage.",
      "cite": ["PMID:41951145"],
      "evidence_ids": ["E4", "E5", "E6", "E7", "E8"]
    },
    {
      "component_id": "C2",
      "text": "By applying this approach to both simulated and experimental fMRI data, the study reported reduced total scan time while achieving a higher signal-to-noise ratio (SNR) and contrast-to-noise ratio (CNR) in regions of interest (ROI). Compared with conventional reconstruction strategies, the proposed method demonstrated potential improvements in activation detection under specific acceleration and encoding conditions, while also providing voxel-wise estimates through a Bayesian framework.",
      "cite": ["PMID:41951145"],
      "evidence_ids": ["E9", "E10"]
    },
    {
      "component_id": "C3",
      "text": "Because of the inherent correlation between Rice's noise and the Magnetic Resonance (MR) signal that occurs during MR image formation, denoising these images is an important but complex process that can prove insufficient and even result in the loss of information details; filtering methods based on Partial Differential Equations (PDEs) have been introduced to overcome these two drawbacks, but the parameters of the filters they propose must be determined with great accuracy. To denoise MR images with high Rice's noise levels, the study proposed adapted filtering anisotropic diffusion PDE filter models using precise local parameter adjustment, which incorporate a robust Rician noise estimator and an unbiased variant of filters based on anisotropic diffusion whose parameters are all calculated simultaneously and optimized adaptively through the introduction of the PSO algorithm and the use of reference-based and no-reference quality metrics (PSNR, SSIM and Blind/Referenceless Image Spatial Quality Evaluator (BRISQUE)); three unbiased anisotropic diffusion filtering models with optimized parameters for Rician noise removing in MR images were proposed.",
      "cite": ["PMID:41903663"],
      "evidence_ids": ["E12", "E13", "E15", "E16", "E11"]
    },
    {
      "component_id": "C4",
      "text": "The various tests performed on synthetic and real MR images provided excellent results compared to some other published methods, thus demonstrating the high performance of the proposed anisotropic diffusion filters in terms of noise reduction and preservation of important details.",
      "cite": ["PMID:41903663"],
      "evidence_ids": ["E17"]
    }
  ],
  "confidence": 0.9,
  "evidence_status": "complete",
  "evidence_gap": ""
}

